SPEERIDAL WAVE FUNCTIONS (p.753)
Wave Equation in Oblate Spheroidal Coordinates
21.5.2
\nabla^{2}+L^{2q}=\frac{\partial}{\partial\xi}[(\xi^{2}+1)\,\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial\xi}]+\frac{\partial}{\partial\eta}[(1-\eta^{2})\,\frac{\partial\Phi}{\partial\eta}]+\frac{\xi^{2}+\eta^{2}}{(\xi^{2}+1)(1-\eta^{2})\partial\phi^{2}}+c^{2}(\xi^{2}+\eta^{2})\Phi=0\]
21.5.2 may be obtained from 21.5.1 by the transformations
\xi\to\pm i\xi, e\to\mp ic.
21.6. Differential Equations for Radial and Angular Prolate Spheroidal Wave Functions
If in 21.5.1 we put
\Phi=R_{m}(e,\xi)S_{m}(e,\eta)\,\sin\, m\phi
then the "radial solution" \(R_{m}(e,\xi)\) and the "angular solution" \(S_{m}(e,\eta)\) satisfy the differential equations
21.6.1
\frac{d}{d\xi}[(\xi^{2}-1)\,\frac{d}{d\xi}\, R_{mn}(e,\xi)]-(\lambda_{mn}-c^{2}\xi^{2}+\frac{m^{2}}{\xi^{2}-1})\, R_{mn}(e,\xi)=0
21.6.2
\frac{d}{d\eta}[(1-\eta^{2})\,\frac{d}{d\eta}\, S_{mn}(e,\eta)]-\frac{\mu^{2}}{\mu^{2}}\, S_{mn}(e,\eta)=0
where the separation constants (or eigenvalues) \(\lambda_{mn}\) are to be determined so that \(R_{mn}(e,\xi)\) and \(S_{mn}(e,\eta)\) are finite at \xi=\pm 1 and \eta=\pm 1 respectively.
(21.6.1 and 21.6.2 are identical. Radial and angular prolate spheroidal functions satisfy the same differential equation over different ranges of the variable.)
Differential Equations for Radial and Angular Oblate Spheroidal Functions
21.6.3
\frac{d}{d\xi}[(\xi^{2}+1)\,\frac{d}{d\xi}\, R_{mn}(e,\xi)]-(\lambda_{mn}-c^{2}\xi^{2}-\frac{m^{2}}{\xi^{2}+1})\, R_{mn}(e,\xi)=0
21.6.4
\frac{d}{d\eta}[(1-\eta^{2})\,\frac{d}{d\eta}\, S_{mn}(e,\eta)]+(\lambda_{mn}+c^{2}\eta^{2}-\frac{m^{2}}{1-\eta^{2}})\, S_{mn}(e,\eta)=0
21.6.3 may be obtained from 21.6.1 by the transformations \xi\to\pm ik, e\to\mp ic; 21.6.4 may be obtained from 21.6.2 by the transformation c\to\mp ic.)
21.7.1
\beta_{mn}^{(1)}(e,\eta)=\sum_{r=0,1}^{\gamma^{\prime}}d\eta^{m}(e)P_{m+r}^{m}(\eta)=-\Pr\text{Polate angular function of the first kind
21.7.2
\beta_{m}^{(2)}(e,\eta)=\sum_{r=-a}^{m}d\eta^{m}(e)Q_{m+r}^{m}(\eta)=-\Pr\text{Polate angular function of the second kind
($P_{m}^{m}(\eta)$ and $Q_{n}^{m}(\eta) are associated Legendre functions of the first and second kinds respectively. However, for --1\leq z\leq 1, P_{m}^{m}(z)=(1-z^{2})^{m/2}d\eta^{m}P_{n}(z)/dz^{m}(see 8.6.6). The summation is extended over even values or odd values of \(r\).)
